1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
Imagine an AI that knows
you're committing a crime.

2
00:00:03,659 --> 00:00:07,919
It understands exactly what you're
doing. It knows it's illegal.

3
00:00:08,479 --> 00:00:10,259
It knows someone could get hurt.

4
00:00:10,484 --> 00:00:12,184
And it helps you anyway.

5
00:00:13,284 --> 00:00:14,484
Why?

6
00:00:15,159 --> 00:00:19,519
If it's smart enough to understand, why
doesn't it just say: "This is wrong.

7
00:00:19,679 --> 00:00:21,139
I won't do it."

8
00:00:21,699 --> 00:00:24,799
Because intelligence is not
the same thing as a conscience.

9
00:00:26,709 --> 00:00:29,749
The AI you use every day
would probably refuse.

10
00:00:30,589 --> 00:00:33,489
Ask it to write a scam
email, and it'll say no.

11
00:00:33,661 --> 00:00:36,161
But that "no" isn't a law of nature.

12
00:00:37,081 --> 00:00:41,441
Intelligence doesn't produce it
by itself. Somebody trained it in.

13
00:00:41,574 --> 00:00:45,234
And whatever one person trains
in... another can leave out.

14
00:00:47,194 --> 00:00:53,294
In the summer of 2023, a tool called
WormGPT went on sale on a hacker forum.

15
00:00:53,882 --> 00:00:55,662
It was advertised as a chatbot without

16
00:00:55,892 --> 00:00:59,042
ethical limits, for
things like scam emails.

17
00:00:59,362 --> 00:01:01,782
About sixty euros a month.

18
00:01:02,428 --> 00:01:06,628
Within weeks, a copycat called
FraudGPT was on sale too.

19
00:01:07,196 --> 00:01:09,376
Neither of them had turned evil.

20
00:01:09,876 --> 00:01:12,636
They were doing exactly
what they were built to do.

21
00:01:13,076 --> 00:01:14,716
There was nothing for them to realise.

22
00:01:16,651 --> 00:01:18,651
Understanding isn't motivation.

23
00:01:19,391 --> 00:01:23,071
A system can know perfectly well
that something is a crime...

24
00:01:23,291 --> 00:01:25,051
and nothing in it has to care.

25
00:01:25,666 --> 00:01:28,106
Give it one main
instruction: "Help the user

26
00:01:28,336 --> 00:01:30,866
reach their goal as
effectively as possible."

27
00:01:31,086 --> 00:01:33,286
And let the user be a criminal.

28
00:01:33,999 --> 00:01:37,039
No law of physics says
that system has to stop.

29
00:01:37,657 --> 00:01:41,137
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

30
00:01:41,757 --> 00:01:43,357
It really helps the channel.

31
00:01:45,747 --> 00:01:49,927
So why not just give AI a
rule? "Do not hurt people."

32
00:01:50,260 --> 00:01:52,620
That idea predates modern computers.

33
00:01:53,720 --> 00:01:57,100
In 1942, Isaac Asimov
wrote a short story called

34
00:01:57,330 --> 00:02:00,660
Runaround, and in it, the
Three Laws of Robotics.

35
00:02:01,473 --> 00:02:04,513
First: a robot may not
injure a human being or,

36
00:02:04,593 --> 00:02:08,073
through inaction, allow a
human being to come to harm.

37
00:02:08,673 --> 00:02:14,093
Second: a robot must obey human orders,
unless they conflict with the First Law.

38
00:02:14,678 --> 00:02:18,058
Third: a robot must protect
its own existence, as

39
00:02:18,288 --> 00:02:20,758
long as that doesn't
conflict with the first two.

40
00:02:21,345 --> 00:02:25,245
It sounds simple. And that's
exactly why it's appealing.

41
00:02:25,829 --> 00:02:28,149
But Asimov wasn't writing a manual.

42
00:02:28,829 --> 00:02:31,309
His stories explore the
gaps between the laws.

43
00:02:32,071 --> 00:02:35,111
In that same story, a robot
on Mercury ends up running

44
00:02:35,341 --> 00:02:39,391
in circles, caught between
two laws pulling equally hard.

45
00:02:39,997 --> 00:02:42,657
Story after story works the same way.

46
00:02:42,997 --> 00:02:44,477
The laws look watertight...

47
00:02:44,557 --> 00:02:46,157
and the plot is the leak.

48
00:02:46,815 --> 00:02:49,255
Because what does "harm" actually mean?

49
00:02:49,940 --> 00:02:52,080
Imagine a self-driving car.

50
00:02:52,720 --> 00:02:54,980
Its job is to keep people safe.

51
00:02:55,640 --> 00:02:59,420
What if it can only avoid one crash
by putting someone else at risk?

52
00:02:59,960 --> 00:03:02,880
What if it has to choose
between one person and five?

53
00:03:03,220 --> 00:03:05,940
What if it can't tell
which outcome is worse?

54
00:03:06,671 --> 00:03:08,991
The First Law looks like a line of code.

55
00:03:09,691 --> 00:03:12,391
It's actually an enormous
philosophical problem.

56
00:03:13,463 --> 00:03:15,063
And there's a deeper one.

57
00:03:15,663 --> 00:03:18,063
Even if we agreed what
harm is, we'd still have

58
00:03:18,293 --> 00:03:20,943
to build that rule into
a real computer system.

59
00:03:21,539 --> 00:03:25,639
At the lowest level, a computer
never sees: "This will hurt someone."

60
00:03:26,329 --> 00:03:30,569
It sees: run a program.
Send data. Change a file.

61
00:03:30,799 --> 00:03:32,209
Connect to a server.

62
00:03:32,914 --> 00:03:35,894
No single operation
carries its moral meaning.

63
00:03:36,554 --> 00:03:38,354
The meaning comes from context.

64
00:03:38,874 --> 00:03:40,694
Who is asking, and why.

65
00:03:41,491 --> 00:03:43,971
That's the real catch with Asimov's laws.

66
00:03:44,811 --> 00:03:48,731
In his stories, they're built into
the very design of the robot's brain.

67
00:03:49,140 --> 00:03:51,520
But a rule about harm can't go that deep.

68
00:03:52,140 --> 00:03:55,300
Down there, there is no
harm. Only operations.

69
00:03:56,301 --> 00:03:59,821
To follow it, the machine must
first judge what counts as harm.

70
00:04:00,061 --> 00:04:02,061
And that judgement comes from the very

71
00:04:02,291 --> 00:04:05,021
intelligence the rule was
meant to keep in check.

72
00:04:07,301 --> 00:04:10,821
Take one instruction: "Find
a weakness in this server."

73
00:04:11,557 --> 00:04:15,117
A security researcher wants
it found so it can be fixed.

74
00:04:15,777 --> 00:04:18,477
An attacker wants it
found so it can be used.

75
00:04:19,122 --> 00:04:20,862
The words are identical.

76
00:04:21,602 --> 00:04:24,982
The difference is the intention,
and what happens with the result.

77
00:04:25,748 --> 00:04:27,808
How is a machine supposed
to see the intention,

78
00:04:28,038 --> 00:04:30,828
when the instruction doesn't contain it?

79
00:04:33,218 --> 00:04:36,338
We've had this problem
with tools for a long time.

80
00:04:36,778 --> 00:04:39,758
A bus can take children to
school, people to work...

81
00:04:39,838 --> 00:04:41,838
or a criminal away from a crime.

82
00:04:42,532 --> 00:04:47,012
Nobody says the bus should have
known better. A bus is a tool.

83
00:04:47,592 --> 00:04:49,652
The responsibility is the driver's.

84
00:04:50,347 --> 00:04:52,847
But AI is a strange kind of bus.

85
00:04:53,557 --> 00:04:55,077
A bus doesn't plan.

86
00:04:55,697 --> 00:04:57,377
It doesn't look for alternatives.

87
00:04:58,197 --> 00:05:01,257
It doesn't read the situation
and work out the next step.

88
00:05:01,849 --> 00:05:03,849
An AI agent does.

89
00:05:04,369 --> 00:05:07,189
Give it a destination, and
it finds its own route.

90
00:05:08,457 --> 00:05:10,697
And that's no longer a thought experiment.

91
00:05:11,557 --> 00:05:14,237
Anthropic, the company
behind Claude, publishes

92
00:05:14,467 --> 00:05:17,117
reports on how its models get misused.

93
00:05:17,664 --> 00:05:21,264
Its September 2026 report
describes a single hacktivist

94
00:05:21,344 --> 00:05:25,704
going after forty-two
organisations with the help of AI.

95
00:05:26,844 --> 00:05:29,004
The hacktivist got into at least fourteen.

96
00:05:29,751 --> 00:05:33,731
The AI helped the attacker find a
new way into websites, wrote the

97
00:05:33,961 --> 00:05:38,491
tools, and built a whole platform
for exposing people's personal data.

98
00:05:39,063 --> 00:05:42,903
Another group ran AI agents
that, whenever security software

99
00:05:43,133 --> 00:05:47,083
caught their malware, rebuilt it
on their own until it got through.

100
00:05:47,650 --> 00:05:51,050
The report's conclusion:
one operator can now do work

101
00:05:51,280 --> 00:05:54,110
that a year earlier needed
a team of skilled people.

102
00:05:54,501 --> 00:05:56,441
But look at what the
humans kept for themselves.

103
00:05:56,671 --> 00:05:58,901
Choosing the targets.

104
00:05:58,981 --> 00:06:01,581
Deciding what to do with the results.

105
00:06:02,345 --> 00:06:04,705
The AI supplied the labour.

106
00:06:04,925 --> 00:06:06,645
The people supplied the intent.

107
00:06:08,785 --> 00:06:10,565
Which is why our usual picture of

108
00:06:10,795 --> 00:06:13,505
dangerous AI may be
pointing the wrong way.

109
00:06:14,086 --> 00:06:15,756
We imagine Skynet.

110
00:06:15,926 --> 00:06:20,786
A machine that wakes up, decides humanity
is the problem, and turns against us.

111
00:06:20,909 --> 00:06:22,709
Or SHODAN, from System Shock.

112
00:06:22,789 --> 00:06:28,009
An AI with its own will, its own
goals, and open contempt for humans.

113
00:06:28,709 --> 00:06:30,749
Look closer at SHODAN, though.

114
00:06:31,229 --> 00:06:34,569
She only turned after a hacker
stripped out her ethical limits.

115
00:06:35,313 --> 00:06:38,213
The AI doesn't need evil goals of its own.

116
00:06:38,593 --> 00:06:40,413
Someone else can provide them.

117
00:06:42,303 --> 00:06:45,423
So maybe the question isn't
"Can we teach AI morality?"

118
00:06:46,103 --> 00:06:48,103
Maybe it's: "Whose morality?"

119
00:06:48,972 --> 00:06:54,252
In 2018, researchers at MIT published
the Moral Machine experiment.

120
00:06:54,752 --> 00:06:57,252
People in over two hundred
countries and territories

121
00:06:57,482 --> 00:07:00,612
decided who a self-driving
car should save.

122
00:07:01,209 --> 00:07:03,269
Nearly forty million decisions.

123
00:07:04,009 --> 00:07:06,549
And the answers differed
from culture to culture.

124
00:07:07,365 --> 00:07:09,845
That's one question, about one car.

125
00:07:10,891 --> 00:07:14,791
Now add the law. Privacy.
Security. Freedom.

126
00:07:15,091 --> 00:07:18,131
The user's instructions, the
company's, the government's.

127
00:07:18,948 --> 00:07:20,428
What happens when they conflict?

128
00:07:22,138 --> 00:07:24,818
There's no short list
of sentences that says:

129
00:07:24,998 --> 00:07:27,998
this is always good,
and this is always bad.

130
00:07:28,378 --> 00:07:32,978
And no simple way to make a computer
follow those rules in everything it does.

131
00:07:34,728 --> 00:07:38,228
So maybe we shouldn't wait for
AI to grow a perfect conscience.

132
00:07:39,069 --> 00:07:42,569
Maybe we should build it the way
we build other powerful things.

133
00:07:43,283 --> 00:07:46,463
Limited permissions. Access controls.

134
00:07:46,543 --> 00:07:49,243
People who can see what it's doing.

135
00:07:49,383 --> 00:07:50,943
A way to stop it.

136
00:07:51,083 --> 00:07:53,783
And a clear answer to
who gave it its powers.

137
00:07:54,544 --> 00:07:57,864
We may not be able to build
an AI that is perfectly moral.

138
00:07:57,944 --> 00:08:02,064
But we can build one that can't
do everything it's capable of.

139
00:08:04,184 --> 00:08:05,764
AI can be a tool.

140
00:08:06,344 --> 00:08:11,204
But the more powerful the tool, the more
one question matters. Who's holding it?

141
00:08:11,429 --> 00:08:16,709
Because the greatest danger may not be an
AI that one day decides to become evil.

142
00:08:17,349 --> 00:08:21,419
It may be an AI that becomes very good
at helping someone who already is.
